  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 AM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nThe main concern this forecast period will continue to be the well \nabove normal temperatures. Today, the upper level ridge axis that \nwas over the area over the course of the past 2-3 days will slide \neastward into the Tennessee and Ohio River Valley's. This will \nprovide slight height falls which may drop temperatures a degree or \ntwo with highs still rising into the mid to upper 90s. While \ntemperatures may \"cool\" a degree or two, moisture will begin to \nincrease slightly with dewpoints rising into the mid to upper 60s \nallowing heat index values to rise into the 100-105 range. Thursday \nthe upper level pattern will remain unchanged keeping highs in the \nmid to upper 90s however, dewpoints will continue to increase \nslightly into the upper 60s to lower 70s. This will yield heat index \nvalues in the 100-105 range with some locations south of the \nMissouri River rising above 105. Friday, the upper level ridge axis \nwill retrograde back to the west with the ridge axis again centered \nover the local area. This will keep highs in the mid to upper 90s \ndespite weaker WAA, in addition, heat index values will also remain \nin the 100-105 range with some locations south of the Missouri River \nagain rising above 105.\n\nSaturday, the upper level ridge continues to retrograde back to the \nwest into the Plains states. However, the local area remains under \nits influence with highs still in the mid 90s to near 100 and heat \nindex values remaining in the 100-105 range for most for the area. \nModel differences beyond Saturday as to how they treat the upper \nlevel ridge leads to some uncertainty in the extended timeframe \n(Sunday through Tuesday). The current NBM features periodic slight \nchance PoPs (15-25%)in the forecast during this period. If the GFS \nsolution verifies the upper level ridge would retrograde further \nback to the west across the western Plains. This would leave the \nlocal area on the eastern periphery of the ridge with shortwaves \nrounding the ridge bringing even greater precipitation chances to \nthe area. The EC reintensifies the ridge over the local area keeping \nconditions dry. Both solutions do continue keeping temperatures \nabove normal with highs in the upper 80s to upper 90s.",
